So a constructor is what the JVM calls automatically every time you create an object with `new`. Three things make it a constructor:
1. Same name as the class
2. No return type — not even `void`
3. Called implicitly by `new`, not by your code

```java
class Person {
    String name;
    int age;

    // Constructor — no return type
    Person(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    // Regular method — has return type
    void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hi, I'm " + name);
    }
}
```

If you accidentally write `void Person(...)`, that becomes a regular method, not a constructor — a common trap in interviews.

If you write a class with no constructors at all, the Java compiler silently inserts a **no-argument constructor** for you. This lets you do `new MyClass()` even though you never wrote a constructor.

```java
class Dog {}
// Compiler inserts: Dog() { super(); }
Dog d = new Dog(); // works fine
```

But the moment you write **any** constructor yourself, the compiler stops providing the default:

```java
class Dog {
    Dog(String name) { /* parameterized */ }
}
Dog d = new Dog(); // COMPILE ERROR — no-arg constructor doesn't exist!
```

To fix this, explicitly add `Dog() {}` back. This is a very common interview gotcha and a real bug that freshers hit often.

Constructor overloading lets you create objects in different ways — with full information, partial information, or defaults. The compiler picks the right constructor based on the number and types of arguments.

```java
class Rectangle {
    int width, height;

    Rectangle() {
        this(1, 1); // delegates to parameterized constructor
    }
    Rectangle(int side) {
        this(side, side); // square
    }
    Rectangle(int width, int height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }
}

new Rectangle();       // 1×1
new Rectangle(5);      // 5×5
new Rectangle(3, 4);   // 3×4
```

Using `this()` to chain constructors avoids duplicating initialization code — the full constructor is the single source of truth.

Constructor chaining with `this()` is about having one constructor call another in the same class, so initialization logic lives in one place.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    String dept;
    double salary;

    Employee(String name) {
        this(name, "General"); // calls 2-arg constructor
    }

    Employee(String name, String dept) {
        this(name, dept, 50000); // calls 3-arg constructor
    }

    Employee(String name, String dept, double salary) {
        // All initialization happens here
        this.name = name;
        this.dept = dept;
        this.salary = salary;
    }
}
```

The **rule**: `this()` must be the very first statement. You cannot write any code before it. Also, you cannot have both `this()` and `super()` in the same constructor — both require being first.

A private constructor means no outside class can do `new MyClass()`. The class controls its own instantiation.

**Use case 1: Singleton Pattern**
```java
class DatabaseConnection {
    private static DatabaseConnection instance;

    private DatabaseConnection() {} // prevent external creation

    public static DatabaseConnection getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new DatabaseConnection();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

**Use case 2: Utility class**
```java
class MathUtils {
    private MathUtils() {} // never meant to be instantiated
    public static int square(int n) { return n * n; }
}
```

Java's `java.lang.Math` is a real-world example of a utility class with a private constructor.

The order of execution when creating an object is:

1. **Static initializer blocks** — run once when the class is first loaded (not per-object)
2. **super() call** — the parent constructor runs first (implicitly added if not written)
3. **Instance initializer blocks** — run before the constructor body, every time an object is created
4. **Constructor body** — the code you write in the constructor

```java
class Animal {
    Animal() { System.out.println("Animal constructor"); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    { System.out.println("Dog instance block"); } // runs before constructor body
    Dog() {
        super(); // implicit if not written — MUST be first
        System.out.println("Dog constructor");
    }
}
new Dog();
// Output:
// Animal constructor
// Dog instance block
// Dog constructor
```
